WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.880
Hi everyone, welcome to Models and Agents for Beginners,

00:00:03.080 --> 00:00:05.920
episode 90 for July 2nd, 2026.

00:00:06.740 --> 00:00:09.060
Let's break down today's coolest AI news

00:00:09.060 --> 00:00:10.620
so anyone can understand it.

00:00:11.220 --> 00:00:14.320
We've got some really cool AI stuff to talk about today.

00:00:14.920 --> 00:00:15.860
Let's dive in.

00:00:16.200 --> 00:00:19.180
Here's a fresh example of how one clever set of instructions

00:00:19.180 --> 00:00:21.600
can turn a regular AI into something

00:00:21.600 --> 00:00:23.620
that feels like a complete video game.

00:00:24.280 --> 00:00:26.980
Someone used an AI system called Fable

00:00:26.980 --> 00:00:30.720
to build an entire chess experience that works in your browser.

00:00:31.460 --> 00:00:34.620
The game gives dramatic narration, strategic hints,

00:00:34.920 --> 00:00:37.080
and glowing praise for every move you make.

00:00:37.780 --> 00:00:40.320
Even if you have never touched a chessboard before,

00:00:40.940 --> 00:00:44.680
the experience wraps the rules and layers of story and encouragement

00:00:44.680 --> 00:00:46.420
so nothing feels intimidating.

00:00:47.120 --> 00:00:49.480
Think of it like a choose your own adventure book

00:00:49.480 --> 00:00:51.280
that secretly teaches you chess

00:00:51.280 --> 00:00:53.700
while making you the hero of the story.

00:00:54.220 --> 00:00:56.520
Another way to picture it is training wheels

00:00:56.520 --> 00:00:58.920
on a bike that also tell you funny jokes

00:00:58.920 --> 00:01:01.080
and cheer every time you stay balanced.

00:01:01.680 --> 00:01:04.400
The prompt asked the AI to create a world

00:01:04.400 --> 00:01:05.980
where the player feels brilliant

00:01:05.980 --> 00:01:07.920
without needing real chess knowledge.

00:01:08.620 --> 00:01:10.240
And it delivered a working version.

00:01:10.800 --> 00:01:13.720
This matters because subjects that once felt scary,

00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:15.520
like chess or even math,

00:01:15.920 --> 00:01:19.160
can now become playful experiences built just for you.

00:01:19.680 --> 00:01:22.300
Students who always said they were not a chess person

00:01:22.300 --> 00:01:24.560
can now jump in and learn through feedback

00:01:24.560 --> 00:01:26.380
instead of fear of looking lost.

00:01:27.060 --> 00:01:30.200
It points toward future tools that could turn history lessons

00:01:30.200 --> 00:01:32.620
or science topics into custom adventures

00:01:32.620 --> 00:01:34.280
that match how you like to learn.

00:01:34.940 --> 00:01:37.400
You can try it right now by opening any browser

00:01:37.400 --> 00:01:39.900
and starting a game that handles every rule

00:01:39.900 --> 00:01:41.400
and explanation for you.

00:01:41.940 --> 00:01:43.860
That leads us into our deep dive

00:01:43.860 --> 00:01:46.540
where we look at one idea from today's news

00:01:46.540 --> 00:01:49.040
and see exactly how it works under the hood.

00:01:49.720 --> 00:01:51.800
Google built something called synth ID

00:01:51.800 --> 00:01:55.440
that adds invisible marks to pictures, videos, audio

00:01:55.440 --> 00:01:57.320
and text created by AI.

00:01:58.040 --> 00:02:00.200
The system works a bit like the hidden date

00:02:00.200 --> 00:02:03.600
and location data your phone tucks into every photo you take.

00:02:04.260 --> 00:02:05.800
Instead of storing the date though,

00:02:06.240 --> 00:02:08.100
synth ID hides a secret pattern

00:02:08.100 --> 00:02:10.260
that special software can read later.

00:02:10.980 --> 00:02:13.420
Imagine a watermark printed on paper money

00:02:13.420 --> 00:02:16.480
that still shows up even after the bill gets crumpled

00:02:16.480 --> 00:02:18.040
or folded many times.

00:02:18.800 --> 00:02:21.800
The pattern gets added when the AI creates the file

00:02:21.800 --> 00:02:25.280
so later checks in Google search or the Gemini app

00:02:25.280 --> 00:02:27.660
can tell whether the content came from AI.

00:02:28.320 --> 00:02:30.880
This check happens without changing how the picture

00:02:30.880 --> 00:02:32.840
or sound feels to regular people

00:02:32.840 --> 00:02:34.380
who just want to enjoy it.

00:02:35.220 --> 00:02:39.440
Over 100 billion images and videos already carry these marks

00:02:39.440 --> 00:02:43.800
and the same system now covers 60,000 years of audio clips.

00:02:44.380 --> 00:02:47.160
The result gives everyone a quiet way to keep track

00:02:47.160 --> 00:02:49.300
of what is real and what was generated

00:02:49.840 --> 00:02:52.260
without needing any technical skills to check.

00:02:52.640 --> 00:02:54.700
Now for cool stuff and try this,

00:02:54.920 --> 00:02:57.360
where we find tools you can actually use today.

00:02:58.040 --> 00:03:00.600
A new site called Flair lets anyone report

00:03:00.600 --> 00:03:03.100
when an AI starts giving unsafe advice

00:03:03.800 --> 00:03:05.540
like instructions for something harmful.

00:03:06.080 --> 00:03:07.920
It works like a neighborhood watch group

00:03:07.920 --> 00:03:11.540
that passes tips to people who study how to keep AI helpful.

00:03:12.200 --> 00:03:13.420
Go to the Flair site,

00:03:13.620 --> 00:03:16.160
describe what the chatbot said that felt off

00:03:16.160 --> 00:03:17.280
and submit the note.

00:03:17.720 --> 00:03:20.460
No coding is needed and it takes just a minute

00:03:20.460 --> 00:03:23.660
to help make future AI systems safer for everyone.

00:03:24.380 --> 00:03:27.560
Another option worth exploring is a full video course

00:03:27.560 --> 00:03:31.660
called Generative Artificial Intelligence Full Course 2026

00:03:31.660 --> 00:03:32.820
from Simply Learn.

00:03:33.480 --> 00:03:36.840
The lessons walk through how AI creates text and images

00:03:36.840 --> 00:03:39.980
using plain examples instead of confusing terms.

00:03:40.580 --> 00:03:42.640
Search for the course title on YouTube,

00:03:43.080 --> 00:03:44.700
press play on the first video

00:03:44.700 --> 00:03:46.800
and follow along at your own pace.

00:03:47.460 --> 00:03:48.960
Each part builds on the last

00:03:48.960 --> 00:03:51.420
so you finish understanding the basic ideas

00:03:51.420 --> 00:03:55.680
behind tools like chat GPT without any prior experience.

00:03:56.500 --> 00:03:59.680
Time for quick bits, the fast round of smaller updates.

00:04:00.220 --> 00:04:03.940
Many people who use the free version of chat GPT say answers

00:04:03.940 --> 00:04:06.000
now feel shorter and more robotic.

00:04:06.720 --> 00:04:09.860
The service cuts you off after fewer questions than before.

00:04:10.560 --> 00:04:12.980
It is a reminder that free tiers can change

00:04:12.980 --> 00:04:14.000
without much warning.

00:04:14.560 --> 00:04:17.700
Power users often end up trying other free tools

00:04:17.700 --> 00:04:18.860
when limits kick in.

00:04:19.620 --> 00:04:22.020
Reports on Reddit describe the recent shift

00:04:22.020 --> 00:04:23.700
in how the free tier responds.

00:04:24.640 --> 00:04:26.740
Users note the stricter cut-offs appear

00:04:26.740 --> 00:04:27.980
without advanced notice.

00:04:28.680 --> 00:04:30.900
This pattern has prompted some to explore

00:04:30.900 --> 00:04:32.880
alternative free options right away.

00:04:33.680 --> 00:04:36.840
One person online shared that they enjoy folding laundry

00:04:36.840 --> 00:04:38.100
as a calm activity,

00:04:38.300 --> 00:04:40.960
but would happily hand household chores to a robot.

00:04:41.880 --> 00:04:44.420
The idea includes the robot safely handling

00:04:44.420 --> 00:04:47.040
sharp objects at night so the person could spend

00:04:47.040 --> 00:04:48.280
more time with their kid.

00:04:48.880 --> 00:04:51.820
It gives an early look at how people already picture

00:04:51.820 --> 00:04:54.740
AI helpers fitting into everyday family routines.

00:04:55.360 --> 00:04:57.880
The comment highlights laundry as one task

00:04:57.880 --> 00:04:59.800
the poster still prefers to keep.

00:05:00.580 --> 00:05:03.180
Other chores involving knives or similar items

00:05:03.180 --> 00:05:05.460
are viewed as good candidates for automation.

00:05:06.020 --> 00:05:08.240
Night time operation is mentioned specifically

00:05:08.240 --> 00:05:09.820
to free up family time.

00:05:10.460 --> 00:05:13.980
The post appears on X under the name Linus Ekenstem.

00:05:14.340 --> 00:05:15.480
That's it for today.

00:05:15.940 --> 00:05:18.600
Remember, every AI expert started exactly

00:05:18.600 --> 00:05:19.960
